# __TITLE__ board

Board: __PROJECT_URL__
Linked repos: __REPOS__ (primary: __PRIMARY_REPO__)
Setup: **__PRESET__** preset, **__PACE__** pace.
<!-- when: wip -->
WIP limit: __WIP__ items In Progress. Over the limit, warn the user. Never block or move their work.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: endDate -->
Ends: __END_DATE__ (countdown in the project README).
<!-- end -->

All project, field, and option IDs are in [config.json](config.json) (a link to `.github/linear-board.json`). Do not rediscover them. Run every command through the shared CLI, which is also used by the GitHub Actions:

```
export LINEAR_BOARD_CONFIG=.agents/skills/__SKILL_NAME__/config.json
bash .github/linear-board/board.sh help
```

Run commands from the root of __PRIMARY_REPO__, with `LINEAR_BOARD_CONFIG` set as above. Below, `board` is short for `bash .github/linear-board/board.sh`. Refs look like `owner/repo#12`, `repo#12`, or `#12`. A bare number refers to the primary repo. `board profile` shows what this board has. A command for a feature the board does not have fails with a message that names the upgrade.

## Model

| Linear | Here |
|---|---|
| Workflow state | `Status`: __STATUSES__ |
<!-- when: self-paced -->
| Now / Next / Later | Now = In Progress<!-- review --> and In Review<!-- /review -->, Next = Todo<!-- backlog -->, Later = Backlog<!-- /backlog --> |
<!-- end -->
| Priority | `Priority`: Urgent, High, Medium, Low, No priority |
<!-- when: estimate -->
| Estimate | `Estimate`: __ESTIMATES__ |
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: cycle -->
| Cycle | `Cycle` iteration, __CYCLE_DAYS__ days. Use `@current`, `@next`, `@last`, or a title such as "Cycle 3" |
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: projects timeboxed -->
| Project | Parent issue labeled `project`, with sub-issues. Lead = assignee. `Target date`, `Health` |
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: projects self-paced -->
| Project | Parent issue labeled `project`, with sub-issues. Lead = assignee. Progress = sub-issues done (no target date or health) |
<!-- end -->
| Blocked by | GitHub issue dependencies (`board block`). Shown on the issue and the board card. A closed blocker no longer counts |
| Type | Label: __TYPES_LIST__ |
<!-- when: canceled -->
| Duplicate | Closed as duplicate, Status = Canceled |
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: !canceled -->
| Duplicate | Closed as duplicate (there is no Canceled status) |
<!-- end -->

Automation that already runs, so do not repeat it by hand:

- New issues with no Status land in **__INBOX__**.
<!-- when: assign -->
- A branch named `12-...` moves #12 to In Progress and assigns the branch creator.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: !assign -->
- A branch named `12-...` moves #12 to In Progress.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: review -->
- An open PR moves it to In Review. A merge moves it to Done.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: !review -->
- A merged PR moves it to Done.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: wip -->
- When an issue moves to In Progress and the board is over the WIP limit, git-sync comments on the issue. It does not block anything.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: cycle-rollover -->
- Ended cycles roll over unfinished items (daily).
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: stale -->
- Backlog items get `stale` after __STALE_DAYS__ days without activity (weekly).
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: sla -->
- Urgent items get `sla-breach` after __SLA_HOURS__h with no activity (hourly).
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: overdue -->
- Items past their due date get `overdue` (hourly).
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: weekly-digest timeboxed -->
- A weekly digest is posted as a project status update (completed, in progress, rolled over, at risk, overdue).
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: weekly-digest self-paced -->
- A weekly "what got done" log is posted as a project status update. It has no status and no deadlines.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: endDate -->
- The README countdown is refreshed whenever git-sync runs.
<!-- end -->

## 1. Triage inbox

<!-- when: triage -->
1. Run `board triage`. It lists Triage items (oldest first) and items labeled `stale`.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: !triage -->
1. Run `board triage`. It lists __INBOX__ items that have no priority yet (oldest first)<!-- stale -->, plus items labeled `stale`<!-- /stale -->.
<!-- end -->
2. For each item, read the issue (`gh issue view N -R repo`). Propose a type, a priority<!-- estimate -->, and an estimate<!-- /estimate -->, then ask the user. Decide one item at a time unless the user wants a batch.
3. Apply the decision:
<!-- when: estimate -->
   - Accept: `board set REF priority=High estimate=3` and then `board accept REF Todo` (or the default, `Backlog`). Add the type label with `gh issue edit N -R repo --add-label bug` if it is missing.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: !estimate -->
   - Accept: `board set REF priority=High`. Add the type label with `gh issue edit N -R repo --add-label bug` if it is missing.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: canceled -->
   - Decline: `board decline REF "reason"`. This sets Canceled and closes the issue as not planned.
   - Duplicate: `board duplicate REF ORIGINAL_REF`.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: !canceled -->
   - Decline: `board decline REF "reason"`. This closes the issue as not planned.
   - Duplicate: `board duplicate REF ORIGINAL_REF`.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: stale -->
   - A stale item that is still relevant: `board accept REF Backlog` (this removes `stale`). Otherwise, decline it.
<!-- end -->
4. End with a summary: how many accepted, declined, and marked duplicate.

## 2. Create an issue, Linear-style

<!-- when: timeboxed -->
Ask only for the values that are missing. Use the defaults: type from context, priority "No priority", no estimate, no cycle.

```
board create --repo REPO --title "..." --body "..." --type __TYPES__ \
  --priority High --estimate 3 --cycle @current --due 2026-12-01 --parent '#4' --assignee @me --blocked-by '#3'
```

Leave out the flags for fields the board does not have (see `board profile`). Status defaults to Todo when a cycle is set, else <!-- backlog -->Backlog<!-- /backlog --><!-- !backlog -->Todo<!-- /!backlog -->. Use `--status __INBOX__` to send the issue to the inbox.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: self-paced -->
Ask only for the values that are missing. Use the defaults: type from context, priority "No priority". There are no cycles<!-- !dueDate --> and no due dates<!-- /!dueDate -->.

```
board create --repo REPO --title "..." --body "..." --type __TYPES__ --priority High \
  <!-- estimate -->[--estimate 3] <!-- /estimate --><!-- dueDate -->[--due 2026-12-01] <!-- /dueDate --><!-- projects -->[--parent '#4'] <!-- /projects -->[--assignee @me] [--status Todo] [--blocked-by '#3']
```

Use only the flags for fields the board has (see `board profile`). Status defaults to <!-- backlog -->Backlog (Later)<!-- /backlog --><!-- !backlog -->Todo<!-- /!backlog -->. Use `--status Todo` for Next.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: projects -->
`--parent` makes the new issue a sub-issue of a project issue.
<!-- end -->
When the issue cannot start until other issues are done, add `--blocked-by '#3,#5'`. For an existing issue, run `board block REF BLOCKER_REF...` (`board unblock` removes links). When you file a batch of related issues, create the blockers first so you can reference them.
Report the URL.

<!-- when: cycle -->
## 3. Cycle planning and review

- Plan: `board plan --cycle @next` shows scope against the average velocity of the last 3 cycles (points completed), plus unscheduled Todo and Backlog candidates by priority. Propose a scope that fits capacity. Check `board show REF` for `blockedBy`: leave out items whose blockers will not finish this cycle. After the user agrees, schedule each item: `board set REF cycle=@next`. If the cycle is over capacity, say which low-priority items to drop.
- Review: `board review --cycle @last` (or a title). It shows completed, canceled, still open, rolled over, and scope added after the cycle start. Summarize it in a few lines and point out patterns (for example, repeated rollovers or large unestimated items).
- List cycles: `board cycles`.
<!-- when: !cycle-rollover -->
- This board has no rollover Action. When a cycle ends, run `board rollover` to move unfinished items into the current cycle.
<!-- end -->
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: self-paced -->
## 3. What's next

Run `board next` (or `board next --limit 10`). It shows:

- **Now**: items In Progress<!-- review --> or In Review<!-- /review -->, and the WIP count against the limit of __WIP__.
- **Next**: open Todo<!-- backlog --> and Backlog<!-- /backlog --> items with no open blockers, ordered by priority (Urgent first), then by age (oldest first).
- **Blocked**: open items that wait on other open issues, with what each one waits on.

Suggest the top Next item. If a Blocked item outranks it, say what it waits on, such as "#14 waits on #13". If the user wants a blocked item anyway, suggest working on its blockers first. When the user starts it, run `board set REF status="In Progress"`. If the board is over the WIP limit, the command prints a warning: tell the user and suggest finishing or pausing something first. Do not refuse to start the item.

To see what got done, run `board digest --dry-run`.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: projects -->

## 4. Projects

- Create: `board project-create --repo REPO --title "..." --lead LOGIN<!-- targetDate --> --target 2026-12-15<!-- /targetDate --><!-- health --> --health "On track"<!-- /health -->`.
- Add work: `board project-add PROJECT_REF CHILD_REF`, or create the child with `board create --parent`.
<!-- when: health -->
- Review: `board projects` shows the lead, target, health, and sub-issue progress.
- Update health: `board set PROJECT_REF health="At risk" target=2027-01-10`.
- Post a project status update (this shows on the board's status panel): `board status-update --status ON_TRACK|AT_RISK|OFF_TRACK|COMPLETE --body "..."`. Write the body from `projects`, `overdue`, and the current cycle. To get a draft of the automatic digest, run `board digest --dry-run`.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: !health -->
- Review: `board projects` shows the lead and sub-issue progress. Progress is the only signal: there is no health or target date.
- Post a project status update (this shows on the board's status panel): `board status-update --body "..."`. Write it as a log of what got done (`board digest --dry-run` gives a draft). Do not add at-risk or deadline language.
<!-- end -->
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: timeboxed full,solo -->

## 5. Overdue and SLA

Run `board overdue`. For each item, suggest an action: reschedule (`board set REF due=...`), raise the priority, reassign, or decline. Ask before you change anything.
<!-- end -->
<!-- when: endDate -->

## Countdown

Run `board readme` to refresh the "N days left" line in the project README.
<!-- end -->

## Label sync

Run `board labels-sync`. It creates or updates this board's labels (__LABELS__) in every linked repo. To add a label everywhere, add it to `labels` in `.github/linear-board.json` in each repo, then run the sync.

<!-- when: hackathon,solo,self-paced -->
## Upgrade the board

Upgrades only add Status options, fields, views, labels, workflows, and issue forms. They never delete anything (views from the old setup stay). Allowed: preset hackathon -> solo -> full, and pace self-paced -> timeboxed.

1. Ask the user which upgrade they want, and for Timeboxed, the cycle length in days and the start (a date or a weekday).
<!-- when: self-paced wip -->
   For Timeboxed, also ask whether to keep the WIP limit of __WIP__, change it to a new number, or remove it. Timeboxed boards do not need one. The command fails without this answer.
<!-- end -->
2. Run, for example: `board upgrade --preset solo` or `board upgrade --pace timeboxed --cycle-days 14 --cycle-start monday`. Use `--wip N` for a new WIP limit, `--wip keep` to keep it, or `--wip none` to remove it.
3. It updates `.github/linear-board.json`, the board, the labels, `.github/workflows`, `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE`, and this skill. Show `git status`, then ask before you commit and push. Scheduled workflows run only from the default branch.
4. Give the user the manual steps that the command prints (built-in workflow inbox status, settings for the new views, other linked repos).
<!-- end -->

## Rules

- Ask before you close, decline, or bulk-edit issues.
- Use the CLI for writes. For something the CLI does not cover, use `gh api graphql` with the IDs from config.json.
- Item JSON for custom queries: `board items --status Todo | jq ...`.
